      "body": "## Purpose\n\nFixes #60172.\n\nOn sm_120 GPUs (RTX PRO 6000, RTX 5090), `TRITON_MLA` is the only MLA backend, and the first decode fails with:\n\n```text\nout of resource: shared memory, Required: 102400, Hardware limit: 101376\n```\n\n`_decode_grouped_att_m_fwd` launches the MLA shape (`BLOCK_DMODEL=512`, `BLOCK_DPE=64`, `BLOCK_N=32`, `BLOCK_H=16`) with `num_stages=2`, which needs 102,400 bytes. These GPUs allow 101,376 bytes per block. The existing one-stage fallback only triggers when `BLOCK_DMODEL >= 1024`, so MLA (latent width 512) never gets it.\n\nThis PR also uses one stage when the device's `max_shared_mem` is below 128 KiB. It reads the value from the Triton driver, cached per device. GPUs with large shared memory (Hopper, datacenter Blackwell) keep two stages, so their behavior is unchanged.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n- The standalone reproduction in #60172 (`decode_attention_fwd`, `is_mla=True`, Lk=576, 32 heads, FP8 KV cache, page size 16, 1,000-token sequences) on an RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition (CC 12.0), comparing the output against a PyTorch reference.\n- End to end: `sarvamai/sarvam-105b-fp8` on 2x RTX PRO 6000, TP=2, `--kv-cache-dtype fp8`, vLLM 0.31.0. Chat completions and streaming.\n\n## Test Result\n\n- Without the change: `OutOfResources: shared memory, Required: 102400, Hardware limit: 101376`.\n- With it: the kernel runs, with max abs error 8.2e-4 against the reference (reference absmax 0.27). The full model serves correctly with `--enforce-eager`.\n- These runs used vLLM 0.31.0 with the same one-stage condition applied. This file is identical on current main.\n- Not tested: CUDA-graph mode, and sm_86/sm_89 (which share the 101,376-byte limit).\n- `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` pass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
